    "id": "2012hawaiiintermediatecourtofappealsstatevcramer",
    "judge_ruling": " The Hawaiian court held that a person may be convicted of simple assault after provocation if he reasonably believed his defense to have been defeated because it did not constitute an unreasonable act under Hawai'i Revised Statutes section 704 3 which sets out the reasonable force standard for criminal convictions in Hawaii The defendants had argued that they were justified in using deadly force on the victim at issue After reviewing the case law of the state and considering each argument individually the court concluded that there was insufficient evidence to establish provocation The main point raised by both parties is whether a person's defense can be deemed unreasonable if it does not constitute an act of reasonable force under section 704 3 1 which reads: \"A person who commits assault shall have been acting in the exercise or protection on behalf of his immediate family to preserve life and dignity and unless such justification is established by a preponderance of circumstances which demonstrate that it was not an unreasonable act no offense has been committed \" Thus one must show both reasonable cause for fear as well as sufficient grounds to justify the force used The defense counsel argued this statute created a strict liability regime where once provocation occurs the state need only prove actual use of deadly force rather than showing",
